  "_provenance": "URL/WMS-layer-name TEMPLATES for Point Blue's 'Our Coast, Our Future' (OCOF) tile/WMS infrastructure — NOT a copy of the flood data itself. Every actual tile image, WMS render, and legend graphic is still fetched live from geo.pointblue.org at request time. This file exists only because OCOF's own live layer catalog (data.pointblue.org/apps/ocof2_flood_map/api/data_sources/ocof10) has no CORS header and can't be fetched cross-origin from this site's JS — geo.pointblue.org itself (the actual tile/WMS/legend server) does send Access-Control-Allow-Origin: *, confirmed directly. So the *naming convention* (which counties/sub-regions exist per topic, the URL/layer-name template with {slr3}/{storm3}/{inlet} placeholders, and each layer's GeoServer style name for its color-scale legend) is captured here once, the same category of hardcoded config as BCDC_WATER_LEVELS in js/map/layers/bcdc-flood-layer.js or NOAA_SLR_SCENARIOS in js/map/layers/noaa-slr-layer.js, just larger and kept as its own JSON file instead of inline. `style` is used two ways: for `imagewms` layers it's the actual WMS rendering style; for `tilexyz` layers the underlying tiles are pre-rendered outside WMS, but OCOF's own catalog still associates each one with a GeoServer style purely for GetLegendGraphic (same style names, verified directly against each item's own legendUri this session). Extracted and verified directly against the live collection this session (2026-09-17). Not exhaustive — covers a representative, verified-working set of counties per topic, not literally every California county; some topics (e.g. Flood Extent, Flood-prone Low Lying, Wave Runup, Min/Max Flooding) only have real published data for specific counties (e.g. San Diego) upstream, not a gap introduced here.",
